---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **7.7% 는 고르게인가 몰려서인가.** (`/clumping`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **우리 대표 수를 우리가 다시 읽는다.** 7.7% 는 평균이고, 평균은 모양을 가린다.
 * ⛔ **대조군을 화면에 같이 낸다** — 같은 몫이 고르게 흩어졌을 때의 모양(이항).
 *    그것 없이 「몰렸다」고 하면 눈대중이다.
 * ⛔ 나라를 줄세우지 않는다. 몰림의 **분포**만 낸다.
 * ⛔ 왜 몰리는지는 이 자료에 **없다.**
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-clumping.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-clumping.json';

const 없는칸 = data.distribution.find((d) => d.korean === 0);
const 넷이상 = data.distribution.filter((d) => d.korean >= 4);
const 넷이상실제 = +넷이상.reduce((s, d) => s + d.observedPc, 0).toFixed(1);
const 넷이상고르게 = +넷이상.reduce((s, d) => s + d.evenPc, 0).toFixed(1);

const TITLE = 'Our headline 7.7% is not one place a week — it is nothing, then several at once';
const DESC =
  `Korean titles hold ${data.worldSharePc}% of Netflix country chart places. Spread evenly that would put at `
  + `least one on ${data.evenAnyPc}% of country-weeks; the real figure is ${data.observedAnyPc}%. `
  + `${없는칸.observedPc}% of country-weeks have none at all, against ${없는칸.evenPc}% if places fell independently.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/clumping">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    We publish one figure more than any other: Korean titles hold {data.worldSharePc}% of the top-10
    places Netflix lists around the world. This page is about what that average hides.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Ten places cannot hold 0.77 of a title</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.whyEvenComparison}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Country-weeks with at least one Korean title</th><th>Share</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">If the same {data.worldSharePc}% fell independently</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.evenAnyPc}%</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Actually observed</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.observedAnyPc}% — {data.cellsWithAny.toLocaleString('en-US')} country-weeks</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Shortfall against independence</td>
            <td class="tot">{(data.clumping * 100).toFixed(1)}% fewer weeks</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>The same share, sitting in far fewer weeks.</b> A reader who takes
      {' '}{data.worldSharePc}% to mean "usually about one" would be wrong about
      {' '}{(data.evenAnyPc - data.observedAnyPc).toFixed(1)} points of the calendar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How many Korean titles a chart holds in one week</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Korean titles in that country-week</th><th>Country-weeks</th>
            <th>Observed</th><th>If independent</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.distribution.filter((d) => d.cells > 0).map((d) => (
            <tr class={d.korean === 0 || d.korean >= 4 ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{d.korean}</td>
              <td>{d.cells.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
              <td class="tot">{d.observedPc}%</td>
              <td>{d.evenPc}%</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Read the two right-hand columns against each other. <b>Empty weeks are commoner than chance
      allows</b> — {없는칸.observedPc}% against {없는칸.evenPc}% — and so are crowded ones: four or more
      Korean titles in a single country-week happen {넷이상실제}% of the time, where independence
      would give {넷이상고르게}%. The middle is what goes missing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>It is not one or two markets doing it</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in">{data.whyPerCountry}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Markets measured (at least {data.minimumWeeks} country-weeks)</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.marketsMeasured}</td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Markets where places are more clumped than independence</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.marketsMoreClumpedThanEven}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart rows read</td><td class="tot">{data.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Country-weeks read</td><td class="tot">{data.cellsMeasured.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Korean places</td><td class="tot">{data.koreanPlaces.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">All places</td><td class="tot">{data.allPlaces.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Every market is compared against a binomial built from <em>its own</em> Korean share, so a
      country that takes very few Korean titles is not counted as clumped merely for being small.
      {' '}<b>{data.marketsMoreClumpedThanEven} of {data.marketsMeasured}</b> still come out more
      clumped than chance.
    </p>
    {/*
      ⛔ 93개 나라를 이름과 함께 뿌리면 그게 **순위표**다. 이 매체가 안 하기로 한 것이다.
      ⭐ 그래서 나라 이름을 빼고 **몰림이 어떻게 흩어져 있나**만 낸다.
         「어디가 제일 몰렸나」가 아니라 「몰림이 흔한가」가 이 절의 물음이다.
    */}
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>How clumped a market is</th><th>Markets</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {[
            { band: 'Less clumped than independence', lo: -9, hi: 0 },
            { band: 'Up to 10% fewer weeks than independence', lo: 0, hi: 0.1 },
            { band: '10–25% fewer', lo: 0.1, hi: 0.25 },
            { band: '25–40% fewer', lo: 0.25, hi: 0.4 },
            { band: '40% or more fewer', lo: 0.4, hi: 9 },
          ].map((b) => (
            <tr class={b.lo === 0.4 ? 'edge' : ''}>
              <td class="nm">{b.band}</td>
              <td class="tot">
                {data.byMarket.filter((m) => m.clumping != null && m.clumping >= b.lo && m.clumping < b.hi).length}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note fine">
      <b>No market is named here on purpose.</b> Ranking 93 countries by how lopsided their Korean
      weeks are would be a league table, and a league table invites the reader to treat position as
      merit — when what is being measured is release timing, chart volatility and catalogue size
      mixed together. Which end a given market sits at belongs in that market's own sheet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/clumping" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why this cannot say why.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>The same {data.worldSharePc}% as everywhere else on this site.</b> This page counts Korean
        titles with the single rule the rest of the site uses, so the {data.koreanPlaces.toLocaleString('en-US')}
        {' '}places here are the same places as on <a href="/world-share">world share</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/world-share">What share of chart places Korean titles hold</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/rank-tells">What a position does not say</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
